WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.550
Who decides access,
and who carries it out?

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.300
The book keeps those apart.

3
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.233
Deciding: business and application owners
decide who should have access and why,

4
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:12.546
the CISO owns the policy
baseline and its exceptions,

5
00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:16.375
and an identity governance office
owns approvals and reviews.

6
00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:20.417
Executing: identity engineering
builds the policies and settings,

7
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:23.664
the help desk handles
assignments under a scoped role,

8
00:00:23.664 --> 00:00:26.912
and security operations
handles alerts and response.

9
00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:29.601
Assuring: the ISSO owns the evidence,

10
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:35.425
the authorizing official owns accepted
risk, and reviewers recertify access.

11
00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:37.234
The rule that holds it together:

12
00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:40.575
the people who execute access
changes never approve them.

13
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.625
That's why the help desk gets a custom
role that manages assignments only.

14
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:49.256
The old guide gave it a role that
could grant consent and act as any app,

15
00:00:49.256 --> 00:00:51.500
so the rule existed only on paper.
